Subject: [PATCH] ksu: selinux_hide must store a struct page *, not page_address()

Opening /sys/fs/selinux/status from any app wedged a Gold core hard enough to
trip the apps watchdog and reboot the phone. Reproduced with Duck Detector,
whose app zygote (uid >= 10000, TIF_SECCOMP set) hits this hook on every
launch; the phone died ~24 s in, every time.

selinuxfs stores a `struct page *` in filp->private_data and consumes it as
one -- security/selinux/selinuxfs.c on 4.9:

    static int sel_open_handle_status(struct inode *inode, struct file *filp)
    {
            struct page *status = selinux_kernel_status_page();
            ...
            filp->private_data = status;            /* struct page * */
    }

    static int sel_mmap_handle_status(struct file *filp, ...)
    {
            struct page *status = filp->private_data;
            ...
            return remap_pfn_range(vma, vma->vm_start,
                                   page_to_pfn(status),   /* <-- here */
                                   size, vma->vm_page_prot);
    }

    static ssize_t sel_read_handle_status(struct file *filp, ...)
    {
            struct page *status = filp->private_data;
            return simple_read_from_buffer(buf, count, ppos,
                                           page_address(status), ...);
    }

my_sel_open_handle_status() stored page_address(data) instead -- a kernel
virtual address where a struct page * is expected. mmap() of that file then
runs page_to_pfn() on a virtual address, producing a nonsense PFN, and
remap_pfn_range() maps it into userspace. Mapping a PFN that is not valid RAM
is what killed the core: on arm64 that surfaces as an imprecise external
abort with interrupts masked, so the CPU stops taking the timer interrupt and
answers no IPI. Nothing can print its stack, which is why this looked for a
long time like a silent reboot with no crash log.

The read path is wrong in the same way -- it would apply page_address() to an
address that has already been translated.

Storing the struct page * is what both callers expect, and it keeps the
feature working: the fake status page is still handed to unprivileged
callers, so the hiding behaviour is unchanged.

Evidence, three trials each, Duck test/deterministic, RAM-booted fresh per
trial, trigger confirmed firing (carrier_spawns=4) on every survival:

  KSU only, unmodified            WEDGED 3/3   (24, 25, 25 s)
  KSU only, selinux_hide disabled SURVIVED 3/3 (259, 258, 258 s)
  KSU only, this fix applied      SURVIVED 3/3 (258, 259, 258 s)

diff --git a/kernel/feature/selinux_hide.c b/kernel/feature/selinux_hide.c
index b47b5311..e7d4964e 100644
--- a/kernel/feature/selinux_hide.c
+++ b/kernel/feature/selinux_hide.c
@@ -135,7 +135,7 @@ static int __nocfi my_sel_open_handle_status(struct inode *inode, struct file *f
 		   ksu_selinux_hide_is_enabled)) {
 		struct page *data = READ_ONCE(fake_status);
 		if (data) {
-			filp->private_data = page_address(data);
+			filp->private_data = data;
 			return 0;
 		}
 	}
